.TH smi-pki-create-server-cert "1" "October 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI PKI Command"
.SH NAME
smi-pki-create-server-cert \- Create a server certificate with one or several host names
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-pki-create-server-cert
.I DIRECTORY
.I ROOT_DOMAIN
.I DOMAIN_NAME
.RI [ ALT_NAME ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-pki-create-server-cert
generates a private key and a signed TLS server certificate that is valid for
\fIDOMAIN_NAME\fR and for every \fIALT_NAME\fR given after it, all in one
\fBsubjectAltName\fR.
It requires that \fBsmi-pki-ca-create\fR(1) has already created the Root CA and
Intermediate CA of \fIROOT_DOMAIN\fR in \fIDIRECTORY\fR.
.PP
This is the command for a server that is reached by more than one name, for example an
internal name and the public name its clients dial.
A client validates the name it dialed against the \fBsubjectAltName\fR list and nothing
else, so every name the server answers to has to be in the same certificate.
With a single name the result is identical to \fBsmi-pki-create-domain-cert\fR(1), which
remains the shorter form for that case.
.PP
The host names have nothing to do with the domain the CA is named by: a CA created for
\fBgintra\fR signs a certificate for \fBsetmy.info\fR without anything further, because
trust comes from the chain and not from the name.
This is why \fIROOT_DOMAIN\fR comes before the names and is not optional here \- the
names have to be the last arguments, as there may be any number of them.
.PP
The process:
.IP 1.
Checks the Intermediate CA of \fIROOT_DOMAIN\fR before anything is generated; when it is
missing, the command names the file it looked for, creates no files and exits with 1.
.IP 2.
Generates a 2048-bit RSA private key (mode \fB600\fR) and public key, named by
\fIDOMAIN_NAME\fR.
.IP 3.
Creates a CSR with \fIDOMAIN_NAME\fR as Common Name and
\fBDNS:\fIDOMAIN_NAME\fB,DNS:\fIALT_NAME\fB,...\fR as \fBsubjectAltName\fR, exact hosts,
no wildcard.
.IP 4.
Signs the CSR with the \fBIntermediate\fR CA key and certificate from the \fBca\fR
subdirectory, never with the Root CA.
The certificate is \fBCA:FALSE\fR with critical \fBkeyUsage\fR
\fBdigitalSignature,keyEncipherment\fR, \fBextendedKeyUsage=serverAuth\fR and the same
\fBsubjectAltName\fR, valid for 825 days.
.PP
The server key, public key, CSR, extension file and certificate are written in
\fIDIRECTORY\fR itself, outside the \fBca\fR subdirectory, all named by
\fIDOMAIN_NAME\fR.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I DIRECTORY
PKI directory: the CA files are read from its \fBca\fR subdirectory, the server files are
written into it.
.TP
.I ROOT_DOMAIN
Root domain given to \fBsmi-pki-ca-create\fR(1), which selects
\fBca/\fIROOT_DOMAIN\fB.intermediate.crt\fR and \fB.intermediate.priv.key\fR.
.TP
.I DOMAIN_NAME
First host name: the Common Name, the name every generated file is called by, and the
first \fBsubjectAltName\fR entry.
.TP
.I ALT_NAME
Each further host name the same server answers to, added to \fBsubjectAltName\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
An MQTT server behind a stream proxy, reached internally as \fBmqtt.gintra\fR and
publicly as \fBsetmy.info\fR, under a CA created for \fBgintra\fR:
.RS
.nf
smi-pki-create-server-cert /var/opt/pki gintra mqtt.gintra setmy.info
.fi
.RE
.PP
The certificate and its key are then \fBmqtt.gintra.crt\fR and
\fBmqtt.gintra.priv.key\fR, and both names validate.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The certificate was created.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong usage, the Intermediate CA was not found, or an OpenSSL step failed.
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
